<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hammir Singh of Mewar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1364</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Rana Hammir Singh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammir-singh">Hammir Singh</a> was a ruler of Mewar in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> who recovered the fort of Chittor around 1326, a generation after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> had sacked it in 1303. He belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guhila-dynasty">Guhila dynasty</a> and took the title of rana. He founded the Sisodia line that ruled Mewar, later from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a>, until 1949.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hammir-singh-of-mewar.jpg" alt="Hammir Singh of Mewar" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rana Hamir Singh · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rana_Hammir_Singh.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
